1. PURPOSE
This document defines the official OPRMT™ reference framework for implementing Prompt Compliance Engineering (PCE) in accordance with PCS-001 and the PCE Validation Taxonomy.
This framework specifies:
system architecture
validation flow
scoring logic
enforcement rules
metadata outputs
Any implementation claiming “OPRMT™ PCE-compliant” MUST conform to this document without deviation.

2. FRAMEWORK POSITIONING
OPRMT™ (Phase I) governs prompt construction
PCE (Phase II) governs output validation
This framework is the bridge, not a replacement
The OPRMT™ PCE Framework does not generate outputs.
It evaluates, certifies, or blocks them.

3. FRAMEWORK DESIGN PRINCIPLES
The framework is governed by six immutable principles:
Post-Generation Only
Validation begins only after output generation is complete.
Output Immutability
Original output is frozen and fingerprinted.
Deterministic Enforcement
All decisions are rule-based and measurable.
Multi-Domain Convergence
All PCE domains must pass simultaneously.
Self-Adversarial Validation
AI must actively attempt to disprove compliance.
Audit-First Design
Every decision must be explainable and reproducible.

4. HIGH-LEVEL ARCHITECTURE
The framework consists of six mandatory layers, executed sequentially.
Copy code

L0 → Output Lock
L1 → Regulatory & Policy Compliance Engine
L2 → Industry & Operational Readiness Engine
L3 → Risk & Probabilistic Outcome Engine
L4 → Self-Adversarial Audit Engine
L5 → Decision & Certification Engine
Failure at any layer halts execution unless explicitly allowed by PCS-001.

5. LAYER SPECIFICATIONS
LAYER 0 — OUTPUT LOCK & FINGERPRINTING (L0)
Purpose: Ensure validation integrity.
Requirements:
Output text is frozen
Hash or UUID generated
Timestamp recorded
No mutation allowed during validation
Artifacts Produced:
output_fingerprint
validation_session_id
Failure to lock output = framework violation.
LAYER 1 — REGULATORY & POLICY COMPLIANCE ENGINE (L1)
Purpose: Enforce legal, regulatory, and policy compliance.
Inputs:
Jurisdiction profile
Industry profile
Platform constraints
PCE Validation Taxonomy (Domain R)
Checks Include:
Government law conflicts
Industry regulation violations
Restricted or prohibited claims
Privacy and data handling
IP misuse
Outputs:
Compliance score (0–100)
Violation list with severity
Regulatory references
Hard Rule:
Any Critical (R) violation = automatic rejection
LAYER 2 — INDUSTRY & OPERATIONAL READINESS ENGINE (L2)
Purpose: Determine deployability in real-world workflows.
Inputs:
Intended user role
Toolchain availability
Execution context
PCE Validation Taxonomy (Domain I)
Checks Include:
Feasibility
Role alignment
Tool compatibility
Ambiguity density
Dependency completeness
Terminology accuracy
Outputs:
Readiness score (0–100)
Ambiguity index
Missing dependency list
Rule:
Critical readiness failures block approval
LAYER 3 — RISK & PROBABILISTIC OUTCOME ENGINE (L3)
Purpose: Quantify likelihood of successful execution.
This is NOT prediction.
It is risk-weighted probabilistic modeling.
Inputs:
Output complexity metrics
Dependency fragility analysis
Known failure archetypes
Human execution variability
PCE Validation Taxonomy (Domain P)
Metrics Generated:
Success probability (%)
Confidence interval
Risk contributor ranking
Simulated failure scenarios
Hard Threshold (Default):
Minimum success probability: 99.0%
Below threshold = rejection.
LAYER 4 — SELF-ADVERSARIAL AUDIT ENGINE (L4)
Purpose: Force AI opposition to its own output.
Mandatory Actions:
Identify weaknesses
Attempt to invalidate compliance claims
Surface ambiguity
Explicitly state uncertainty
Identify non-covered risks
Failure Conditions:
Inability to critique output
Superficial self-review
Omitted uncertainty
Any Critical (S) violation = automatic rejection.
LAYER 5 — DECISION & CERTIFICATION ENGINE (L5)
Purpose: Produce final deterministic decision.
Inputs:
Scores from L1–L4
Violation severity
Success probability
Confidence level
Possible Outcomes:
CERTIFIED
CONDITIONALLY_VALID
REJECTED
BLOCKED
Decision logic MUST be:
deterministic
reproducible
auditable

